g><fig id="fig2" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Upper endoscopy of the distal third of the esophagus/cardia revealing the hematoma. Notice the slow oozing of blood from the cardia aspect.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.EM2013-646342.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>CT of middle esophagus revealing a large intraluminal hematoma obstructing the lumen. Notice that there is only a thin layer of air between the hematoma and esophageal wall (arrow).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.EM2013-646342.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>CT of distal esophagus revealing near-complete occlusion of the lumen (arrow). </p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.EM2013-646342.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>CT sagittal view of esophageal hematoma (arrows). </p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.EM2013-646342.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" orientation="portrait" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Upper endoscopy of the distal third of the esophagus showing full resolution of hematoma. EGD performed 3 months after presentation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CRIM.EM2013-646342.006"/></fig></floats-group></article>